# GoldenCheetah OpenData

Anonymised training data shared by users of the [GoldenCheetah](https://www.goldencheetah.org)
desktop application between 2018 and 2020: {{n_athletes}} athletes, {{n_activities}} activities,
~{{n_rows}} samples (mostly one per second) of power, heart rate, cadence, speed/distance and altitude.

This is a Parquet conversion of the original release on the Open Science Framework,
[osf.io/6hfpz](https://osf.io/6hfpz/) (DOI [10.17605/OSF.IO/6HFPZ](https://doi.org/10.17605/OSF.IO/6HFPZ)).
No records were removed and no information was lost: every value in the original CSV and JSON
files can be recovered exactly (see *Exactness* below).

## Subsets

| subset | rows | one row per | what |
|---|---|---|---|
| `activities` (default) | {{n_activities}} | activity | sport, start time, data-availability flags and ~380 GoldenCheetah metrics (TSS, NP, CP estimates, zones, mean-max power curve, ...) |
| `athletes` | {{n_athletes}} | athlete | gender, year of birth, counts, provenance (OSF file id + sha256 of the source zip) |
| `timeseries` | ~{{n_rows}} | sample | `time_s, distance_m, power_w, hr_bpm, cadence_rpm, altitude_m` as recorded, mostly 1 Hz; one file per athlete |
| `raw_json` | {{n_athletes}} | athlete | the original per-athlete JSON file, verbatim |
| `timeseries_exceptions` | tiny | value | the few source values outside the Float32 range (see below) |

Join keys: `athlete_id` (UUID, identical to the OSF zip name) and `activity_id`
(the original CSV name without `.csv`, i.e. the *local* start time `YYYY_MM_DD_HH_MM_SS`;
activities without a CSV use their UTC start time followed by `Z`). Other GoldenCheetah
datasets on Hugging Face use the same keys, so they join directly to `activities` and `timeseries` here.

## Usage

```python
import polars as pl

# one athlete = one file: timeseries/athlete_id=<uuid>/part.parquet
ts = pl.read_parquet("hf://datasets/{{repo_id}}/data/timeseries/"
                     "athlete_id={{example_athlete_id}}/part.parquet")

# many athletes: scan the folder and select only the columns you need
power = (pl.scan_parquet("hf://datasets/{{repo_id}}/data/timeseries/*/*.parquet")
           .select("athlete_id", "activity_id", "time_s", "power_w"))

acts = pl.read_parquet("hf://datasets/{{repo_id}}/data/activities/*.parquet",
                       columns=["athlete_id", "activity_id", "sport", "start_time_utc", "coggan_tss"])
```

```sql
-- DuckDB
SELECT sport, count(*) FROM 'hf://datasets/{{repo_id}}/data/activities/*.parquet' GROUP BY ALL;
```

```python
from datasets import load_dataset
ds = load_dataset("{{repo_id}}", "timeseries", streaming=True)
```

## Schema notes

**timeseries**: `time_s` (seconds since start, the CSV `secs`; fractional for a few
sub-second devices), `distance_m` (cumulative distance in metres; the CSV `km` × 1000),
`power_w` (CSV `power`), `hr_bpm` (CSV `hr`), `cadence_rpm` (CSV `cad`), `altitude_m` (CSV `alt`).
Column names carry their unit and are shared across the GoldenCheetah datasets on Hugging Face. Hive layout
`timeseries/athlete_id=<uuid>/part.parquet`; `athlete_id` is also a column inside each file. Missing sensor = null. Rows are as recorded:
gaps (auto-pause, smart recording) are **not** filled. Rows of one activity are contiguous and
in recorded order, which is not always `time_s` order: `time_s` repeats now and then, and in a
very few broken recordings it jumps backwards while distance shows the rows are in order.

*Exactness.* GoldenCheetah exported every number with 6 significant digits. Float32 holds any
6-significant-digit decimal exactly, so the columns are Float32 and nothing is rounded:
formatting a value with `%.6g` gives back the number in the original CSV (for `distance_m`:
the original `km` with the decimal point moved). Casting to Float64 shows binary noise in the
8th digit (`72.5636` → `72.56359863`); round to 6 significant digits if you need the exact
decimal. The handful of source values that lie outside the Float32 range (sub-normal junk such
as `4.94066e-324`, written as `0` here) are listed with their exact Float64 value in
`timeseries_exceptions` (`athlete_id, activity_id, row_in_activity, column, source_value`).

**activities**: `start_time_utc` comes from the JSON; `utc_offset_minutes` is derived by
matching it to the local-time CSV name. `sport` is free text as entered in GoldenCheetah
(`Bike`, `bike`, `Rennrad`, `VirtualRide`, empty, ...). `data_flags` is GoldenCheetah's
15-character availability string (`T`ime, `D`istance, `S`peed, `P`ower, `H`eart rate,
`C`adence, ...). Metric values given by GoldenCheetah as `[value, weight]` pairs are split into
`<metric>` and `<metric>__weight`; array metrics (`power_mmp`, `*_dist`, `*_dist_bins`) are
lists. `has_timeseries` is false for activities without samples (manual entries, days off, ...).

